>
  <p>
    Fences fail slowly and then suddenly. A post that has been leaning for a year goes over in the first hard wind, and
    a gate that scrapes stops latching. This page is a diagnostic guide: what the common failures look like, what usually causes
    them in a sunny, windy, dry climate, and how to decide between a fix and a replacement.
  </p>

  <h2>Common problems and what they usually mean</h2>
  <h3>A leaning or wobbling post</h3>
  <p>
    The post is the foundation, so a lean is a foundation problem. Causes include a post set too shallow or without enough
    concrete, a rotted base on a wood post, or soil that has washed out from irrigation or runoff. If one post leans and
    the rest are firm, resetting or replacing that post is the usual repair. If posts lean along much of the run, the original installation is the problem and
    replacement deserves a real look.
  </p>

  <h3>Wind damage</h3>
  <p>
    Solid privacy panels catch wind like sails. Wind damage tends to show up as panels blown out of their rails, posts
    snapped or pushed over, and gates ripped off their hinges. Document damage with dated photos before cleanup, and if
    you plan to file a claim, call your insurer before you repair. A repair that reuses the same weak post spacing may fail
    again in the next storm, so ask the repair company whether posts need to be closer, deeper, or better set.
  </p>

  <h3>Rotted, warped, or split boards on a wood fence</h3>
  <p>
    Boards fail first at the ground and at the top edge. Individual boards are cheap to replace. When rot has reached
    the rails or posts, the cost climbs because the structure has to be repaired, not just the surface. Sealing or staining on
    a schedule slows this considerably, so a repair is also the moment to ask about a maintenance plan.
  </p>

  <h3>Sun-damaged vinyl</h3>
  <p>
    Vinyl in strong sun can fade, chalk, or become brittle over time, depending on the formulation. Cracked panels
    or rails are usually replaced as sections rather than patched. If your fence is under a manufacturer warranty, read what it says about fading and cracking before you pay for a repair.
  </p>

  <h3>Sagging or sticking gates</h3>
  <p>
    A gate that drags or will not latch is usually a hardware, post, or frame issue rather than a "bad gate." Tightening or replacing
    hinges, resetting the gate post, or adding a diagonal brace or tension cable often solves it. Gate repairs typically cost {repair.gate}.
  </p>

  <h3>Chain-link and ornamental iron</h3>
  <p>
    Chain-link sags where posts lean or the tension wire loosens, and gets bent where it was hit. Ornamental iron fails at
    welds and at the finish: rust starts where the coating is chipped, and a repair usually means welding and recoating.
    Ask whether a repair includes rust treatment and repainting so the fix does not rust through again.
  </p>

  <h3>Cracked or leaning block walls</h3>
  <p>
    Hairline cracks in mortar joints are common and often cosmetic. A crack that widens, runs stair-step through the
    joints, or shows offset between courses suggests movement. A wall that leans or bows, and especially a retaining wall,
    should be assessed by a qualified professional because a failure can be unsafe. A retaining wall of 4 feet or more, or any wall in a front setback,
    involves St. George City Code §10-18-5, so a rebuild may need a permit.
  </p>
  <div class="sgfw-permit">
    <strong>Do not ignore a leaning retaining wall.</strong> If soil is moving or the wall is visibly bowing, keep people
    and structures back from it and get it assessed before the next heavy rain.
  </div>

  <h2>Repair or replace?</h2>
  <p>A useful way to decide is to ask three questions:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>How much of the fence is affected?</strong> A few boards, one gate, or one or two posts point to repair. Widespread rot, lean, or panel failure points to replacement.</li>
    <li><strong>Why did it fail?</strong> If the cause was one event, such as a car strike or a fallen limb, repair is often sensible. If the cause was age or installation, more failures are coming.</li>
    <li><strong>What do the two quotes say?</strong> Ask for a repair quote and a replacement quote on the same scope. When repair gets close to the price of new, replacement usually wins.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What repairs cost</h2>
  <p>These are national ranges published in early 2026, not local quotes. The <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> lists sources and method.</p>
