  <p>Labor for repair work is commonly quoted at {repair.hourly} per hour; welding and specialty work runs higher.</p>

  <h2>Stopping the next failure</h2>
  <p>
    A repair that ignores the cause is a repair you will pay for twice. Before you approve the work, ask the company what
    caused the failure and what they are changing so it does not repeat. The answers that usually matter:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Post depth and setting.</strong> Are replacement posts set deeper, or in concrete, when the old ones failed at the ground?</li>
    <li><strong>Water.</strong> Sprinklers that hit a fence every morning rot wood, corrode metal, and undermine posts. Redirect them.</li>
    <li><strong>Wind load.</strong> On solid fences, ask whether panel spacing, post spacing, or added bracing will help.</li>
    <li><strong>Finish.</strong> Reseal, restain, or recoat while the repair is open. Bare wood and chipped metal finish fail first.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardware.</strong> Replace worn hinges and latches with better-rated hardware rather than reusing them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Safety and temporary fixes</h2>
  <p>
    If a fence is down and it kept a pet, a child, or a pool area secure, put a temporary barrier up right away, even if it is
    just a few sturdy stakes and heavy-duty fencing, while you wait for a repair. Do not stand under or push on a leaning wall,
    and keep your hands clear of broken metal and exposed nails. If a downed fence is tangled with a utility line, stay away and call the utility.
  </p>

  <h2>Before you hire a repair company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Photograph the damage and note when and how it happened.</li>
    <li>Ask for a written scope: what is being replaced, what materials, and whether the fix addresses the cause.</li>
    <li>Check license status yourself with the <a href="https://dopl.utah.gov/" rel="noopener">Utah Division of Professional Licensing</a>, and confirm insurance directly.</li>
    <li>Ask about the warranty on repair work and on any new materials.</li>
    <li>Call 811 (<a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">Blue Stakes of Utah</a>) before any digging, including resetting posts; the service asks for at least 3 business days of notice.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Repair costs are national ranges, not St. George quotes. Permit references cite St. George City Code §10-18-5, rechecked
    September 26, 2026.
  </p>
